* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* SerialTap — behavioural building blocks for the power-path decks
*
* These are BEHAVIOURAL models, not vendor models. They describe the terminal
* behaviour the datasheets promise, at the timescales the decks care about
* (100 us .. 1 s). They deliberately contain no internal control loop, no
* switching and no thermal model.
*
* Where a vendor SPICE model exists for the part finally chosen, the vendor
* model replaces the block here - see sim/README.md "Model fidelity".
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

* --- CLIM: current-limited pass element -------------------------------------
* Below the limit it is a resistor of RON. Above it, a constant-current source
* of ILIM. Implemented as a VCCS on the smooth minimum of the two, so there is
* no algebraic loop and Newton converges without help.
*
* Models both the appliance's own current-limited 5 V rail and the board's
* eFuse (TPS2553 class) in its constant-current region.
*
*   ILIM  A    current limit
*   RON   ohm  pass resistance below the limit (includes source impedance
*              when this block stands in for the appliance supply)
*   SOFT  A    width of the knee between CV and CC. Numerical only; keep it
*              small against ILIM but not so small the solver chatters.
.subckt CLIM in out ILIM=1 RON=0.1 SOFT=1m
Blim in out I = 0.5*( (V(in)-V(out))/{RON} + {ILIM}
+                   - sqrt( ((V(in)-V(out))/{RON} - {ILIM})**2 + {SOFT}**2 ) )
Rpar in out 100Meg   ; DC path around the current source; negligible in circuit
.ends CLIM

* --- IDEALD: ideal-diode ORing controller ------------------------------------
* LM66100 / MAX40203 class. Vfwd is the regulated forward drop, Ron the pass
* FET. Roff is the reverse blocking that keeps a bench USB supply out of the
* appliance's rail (FR-6).
.model IDEALD D(Ron=0.079 Roff=10Meg Vfwd=0.020 epsilon=0.003)

* --- CBULKX: bulk capacitor with ESR -----------------------------------------
* Polymer/hybrid aluminium. ESR is the flat-band value from the datasheet;
* no frequency dependence, which is right for the ms-scale events here.
.subckt CBULKX p n C=470u ESR=25m
Resr  p   mid {ESR}
Cbulk mid n   {C}
Rleak mid n   100Meg   ; DC path for the internal node; keeps LTspice quiet
.ends CBULKX

* --- BUCKLOAD: synchronous buck seen from its input --------------------------
* A constant-POWER load, which is the whole point of modelling it at all: as
* the 5 V rail sags the input current RISES, so sag is regenerative in a way a
* resistive or constant-current stand-in would hide.
*
* Shuts off below VUV, smoothly, over UVW volts. VUV here is a modelling
* floor - deliberately set BELOW any candidate buck's real UVLO so a deck
* reports the true depth of a sag instead of clipping at the threshold it is
* being judged against. The requirement threshold is applied when reading the
* results, not inside the model.
* Control input CTL is a node whose VOLTAGE is the 3.3 V-side load CURRENT
* (1 V = 1 A) - it lets one PULSE source drive the C3's current profile.
*
*   VOUT  V    regulated output
*   ETA        efficiency at the operating point
*   VUV   V    input voltage below which the converter stops
.subckt BUCKLOAD in gnd ctl VOUT=3.3 ETA=0.90 VUV=4.0 UVW=0.05
Bin in gnd I = {VOUT}*V(ctl)/({ETA}*max(V(in,gnd),{VUV}))
+              * (0.5 + 0.5*tanh( (V(in,gnd)-{VUV})/{UVW} ))
.ends BUCKLOAD
